This .rmd file gives you access to two datasets called delivery_data and user_data, which gather information about a coupon-discount campaign run by a food-delivery company. Data are collected during a one-month window across different cities. A share of the user received a discount coupon at the beginning of the month. The company needs to understand if the campaign has been effective from a business perspective.

head(user_data)

# A tibble: 6 × 3
  user_id   age city  
  &lt;chr&gt;   &lt;dbl&gt; &lt;chr&gt; 
1 458        32 Torino
2 1355       31 Udine 
3 52         43 Torino
4 545        53 Udine 
5 1027       41 Torino
6 1430       54 Torino

head(delivery_data)

# A tibble: 6 × 6
  user_id device  coupon    n_orders total_spent avg_delivery_time
  &lt;chr&gt;   &lt;chr&gt;   &lt;fct&gt;        &lt;dbl&gt;       &lt;dbl&gt;             &lt;dbl&gt;
1 1323    mobile  no_coupon        0         0                32.8
2 1158    mobile  coupon           5        86.0              29.9
3 1351    mobile  no_coupon        0         0                37.7
4 1317    mobile  coupon           0         0                46.1
5 587     desktop coupon           2        51.6              29.2
6 443     mobile  coupon          10       190.               35.9


Each row of the dataset represents a user. Columns denote:

user_id: the unique identifier assigned to the user;
city: city of reference for the specific user
device: user’s most used device;
age: age of the user;
coupon: denotes if the user has received the coupon or not;
n_orders: number of orders placed by the user during the month;
total_spent: total amount of money spent by the user during the month;
avg_delivery_time: average delivery time to the user;

Data exploration

Q1. and Q2.

delivery_data |&gt; 
  summarise(
    n_coupon = sum(coupon=="coupon"),
    share_coupon = mean(coupon=="coupon")
  )

# A tibble: 1 × 2
  n_coupon share_coupon
     &lt;int&gt;        &lt;dbl&gt;
1      462        0.308

delivery_data |&gt; 
  left_join(user_data) |&gt; 
  group_by(city) |&gt; 
  summarise(
    n_coupon = sum(coupon=="coupon"),
    share_coupon = mean(coupon=="coupon")
  )

Joining with `by = join_by(user_id)`


# A tibble: 6 × 3
  city    n_coupon share_coupon
  &lt;chr&gt;      &lt;int&gt;        &lt;dbl&gt;
1 Milano        79        0.329
2 Padova        47        0.288
3 Torino       148        0.333
4 Treviso       29        0.29 
5 Trieste      122        0.282
6 Udine         37        0.311




Q3.

delivery_data |&gt; 
  mutate(any_order = n_orders &gt;0) |&gt; 
  group_by(coupon) |&gt; 
  summarise(
    any_order = mean(any_order)
  )

# A tibble: 2 × 2
  coupon    any_order
  &lt;fct&gt;         &lt;dbl&gt;
1 no_coupon     0.641
2 coupon        0.777




Q4.

delivery_data |&gt; 
  left_join(user_data) |&gt; 
  mutate(any_order = n_orders &gt;0) |&gt; 
  mutate(over35 = age&gt;35) |&gt; 
  group_by(over35) |&gt; 
  summarise(
    share_any_order = mean(any_order)
  )

Joining with `by = join_by(user_id)`


# A tibble: 2 × 2
  over35 share_any_order
  &lt;lgl&gt;            &lt;dbl&gt;
1 FALSE            0.684
2 TRUE             0.681

delivery_data |&gt; 
  left_join(user_data) |&gt; 
  mutate(any_order = n_orders &gt;0) |&gt; 
  mutate(over35 = age&gt;35) |&gt; 
  group_by(over35, coupon) |&gt; 
  summarise(
    share_any_order = mean(any_order)
  )

Joining with `by = join_by(user_id)`
`summarise()` has grouped output by 'over35'. You can override using the
`.groups` argument.


# A tibble: 4 × 3
# Groups:   over35 [2]
  over35 coupon    share_any_order
  &lt;lgl&gt;  &lt;fct&gt;               &lt;dbl&gt;
1 FALSE  no_coupon           0.638
2 FALSE  coupon              0.789
3 TRUE   no_coupon           0.644
4 TRUE   coupon              0.764

delivery_data |&gt; 
  left_join(user_data) |&gt; 
  mutate(any_order = n_orders &gt;0) |&gt; 
  mutate(over35 = age&gt;35) |&gt; 
  group_by(over35, coupon) |&gt; 
  summarise(
    share_any_order = mean(any_order)
  ) |&gt; 
  ggplot(aes(x=coupon, y=share_any_order))+
  facet_grid(~over35)+
  geom_col()

Joining with `by = join_by(user_id)`
`summarise()` has grouped output by 'over35'. You can override using the
`.groups` argument.











Q5.

delivery_data |&gt; 
  filter(n_orders&gt;0) |&gt; 
  group_by(coupon) |&gt; 
  summarise(
    average_expenditure = mean(total_spent),
    median_expenditure = median(total_spent)
  )

# A tibble: 2 × 3
  coupon    average_expenditure median_expenditure
  &lt;fct&gt;                   &lt;dbl&gt;              &lt;dbl&gt;
1 no_coupon                97.0               56.5
2 coupon                  116.                69.5




Q6.

delivery_data |&gt; 
  filter(n_orders&gt;0) |&gt; 
  mutate(heavy_user = n_orders&gt;5) |&gt; 
  group_by(coupon) |&gt; 
  summarise(
    share_heavy_users = mean(heavy_user)
  )

# A tibble: 2 × 2
  coupon    share_heavy_users
  &lt;fct&gt;                 &lt;dbl&gt;
1 no_coupon             0.167
2 coupon                0.281

